{
  "requirement": "When authoring evidence for a requirement, its complete fenced declaration MUST\nbe included in the mandatory input set defined by\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** The requirement is implemented and tested well enough. I found no gaps.\n\n**Requirement:** when authoring evidence for a requirement, its complete fenced declaration MUST be in the mandatory input set defined by `requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`.\n\n**Implementation** (`src/authoring/inputs.rs`, `AssessmentSources::prepare`, which carries `{impl}` for `requirements.authoring_input`):\n- The `selectors` list starts with `InputSelector::Requirements { requirements: subject_name }`. This is the subject's own declaration and is always the first selector.\n- Each selector is resolved through `source_input`. For `Requirements` selectors, `InputSelector::resolve` in `src/inputs.rs` returns the matched definition's `raw_lines`. These are the complete raw fenced declaration, including the opening and closing `:::` lines.\n- The only text left out is the `:impl:` and `:test:` option lines. The book says these are not part of the requirement text (the \"Anchors\" section of `md/reference.md`). The anchored sources are supplied as separate inputs.\n- `generated_evidence_selector` returns false for `Requirements` selectors. The `evidence/` exclusion and earlier evidence therefore cannot drop the subject's declaration.\n- The subject input is pushed first in `mandatory`, then deduplicated with the other contributions, and `mandatory` is supplied through `mandatory_inputs()`. Access to the declaration's file goes through `access.file`, so read denial and git-staging checks apply under `requirements.authoring.read_denial`.\n- No behavior in `prepare` goes beyond what the requirement and its sibling requirements specify. The other `{impl}` markers on `prepare` cover the other contributions: references, anchors, terms, built-ins and the reference-set digest. The helper functions are marked for `mandatory_inputs` and related requirements.\n\n**Tests:**\n- `bundle_combines_direct_references_terms_and_current_existing_selectors` is marked `{test}` for this requirement. It prepares a subject that has existing evidence and checks that old inputs are not carried forward. For the example subject it asserts that `mandatory_inputs()[0][\"requirements\"] == \"requirements.case\"`. That is the same code path, because the subject selector is added regardless of kind.\n- `mandatory_git_inputs_require_staging_declarations_sources_and_terms` is also marked `{test}`. It checks that preparation fails and names `md/requirements.md` when the declaration file is unstaged. This is the failure case for an unresolvable declaration input.\n- Other tests in the same module check the requirement subject's declaration directly, although they are not marked `{test}` for this requirement:\n  - `unresolvable_earlier_inputs_do_not_block_reassessment` and `anchored_files_contribute_root_node_inputs` assert the exact full fenced text `[\":::{req} ready\", \"The project MUST be ready.\", \":::\"]`.\n  - `only_edges_leaving_the_subject_contribute_inputs` asserts that `requirements.ready` is present.\n- Together these cover the declaration with and without existing evidence, with anchors, with unrelated and unresolvable earlier inputs, and the failure case.\n\n**Remaining gap:** none that affects the verdict. The marked tests assert the declaration's presence for the example subject only. For requirement subjects the assertions are in the unmarked tests above. I judged this adequate because the logic is a single shared, trivially simple selector push.",
  "references": "5d2d6c1d5124873b31ec710480f9a986971d91d25ee9fa8e7f0ee6c5a4b91f2a",
